eslint/no-unsafe-finally Correctness ​✅ This rule is turned on by default. What it does ​Disallow control flow statements in finally blocksWhy is this bad? ​JavaScript suspends the control flow statements of try and catch blocks until the execution of finally block finishes. So, when return, throw, break, or continue is used in finally, control flow statements inside try and catch are overwritten, which is considered as unexpected behavior.Examples ​Examples of incorrect code for this rule:javascript// We expect this function to return 1;
(() => {
  try {
    return 1; // 1 is returned but suspended until finally block ends
  } catch (err) {
    return 2;
  } finally {
    return 3; // 3 is returned before 1, which we did not expect
  }
})();

// > 3How to use ​To enable this rule in the CLI or using the config file, you can use:CLIConfig (.oxlintrc.json)bashoxlint --deny no-unsafe-finallyjson{
  "rules": {
    "no-unsafe-finally": "error"
  }
}References ​Rule Source

