typescript/no-extra-non-null-assertion Correctness ​✅ This rule is turned on by default. What it does ​Disallow extra non-null assertions.Why is this bad? ​The ! non-null assertion operator in TypeScript is used to assert that a value's type does not include null or undefined. Using the operator any more than once on a single value does nothing.Examples ​Examples of incorrect code for this rule:tsconst foo: { bar: number } | null = null;
const bar = foo!!!.bar;tsfunction foo(bar: number | undefined) {
  const bar: number = bar!!!;
}tsfunction foo(bar?: { n: number }) {
  return bar!?.n;
}Examples of correct code for this rule:tsconst foo: { bar: number } | null = null;
const bar = foo!.bar;tsfunction foo(bar: number | undefined) {
  const bar: number = bar!;
}tsfunction foo(bar?: { n: number }) {
  return bar?.n;
}How to use ​To enable this rule in the CLI or using the config file, you can use:CLIConfig (.oxlintrc.json)bashoxlint --deny typescript/no-extra-non-null-assertionjson{
