oxc/no-accumulating-spread Perf ​What it does ​Prevents using object or array spreads on accumulators in Array.prototype.reduce() and in loops.Why is this bad? ​Object and array spreads create a new object or array on each iteration. In the worst case, they also cause O(n) copies (both memory and time complexity). When used on an accumulator, this can lead to O(n^2) memory complexity and O(n^2) time complexity.For a more in-depth explanation, see this blog post by Prateek Surana.Examples ​Examples of incorrect code for this rule:javascriptarr.reduce((acc, x) => ({ ...acc, [x]: fn(x) }), {});
Object.keys(obj).reduce((acc, el) => ({ ...acc, [el]: fn(el) }), {});

let foo = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) foo = [...foo, i];Examples of correct code for this rule:javascriptfunction fn(x) {
  // ...
}

arr.reduce((acc, x) => acc.push(fn(x)), []);
Object.keys(obj).reduce((acc, el) => {
  acc[el] = fn(el);
}, {});
// spreading non-accumulators should be avoided if possible, but is not
// banned by this rule
Object.keys(obj).reduce((acc, el) => {
  acc[el] = { ...obj[el] };
  return acc;
}, {});

let foo = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) foo.push(i);How to use ​To enable this rule in the CLI or using the config file, you can use:CLIConfig (.oxlintrc.json)bashoxlint --deny oxc/no-accumulating-spreadjson{
  "rules": {
    "oxc/no-accumulating-spread": "error"
  }
}References ​Rule Source

