import/no-duplicates Style ​What it does ​Reports if a resolved path is imported more than once in the same module. This helps avoid unnecessary duplicate imports and keeps the code clean.Why is this bad? ​Importing the same module multiple times can lead to redundancy and unnecessary complexity. It also affects maintainability, as it might confuse developers and result in inconsistent usage of imports across the code.Examples ​Examples of incorrect code for this rule:javascriptimport { foo } from "./module";
import { bar } from "./module";

import a from "./module";
import { b } from "./module";Examples of correct code for this rule:typescriptimport { bar, foo } from "./module";

import * as a from "foo"; // separate statements for namespace imports
import { b } from "foo";

import { c } from "foo"; // separate type imports, unless
import type { d } from "foo"; // `preferInline` is trueHow to use ​To enable this rule in the CLI or using the config file, you can use:CLIConfig (.oxlintrc.json)bashoxlint --deny import/no-duplicates --import-pluginjson{
  "plugins": ["import"],
  "rules": {
    "import/no-duplicates": "error"
  }
}References ​Rule Source

