jest/max-expects Style ​What it does ​As more assertions are made, there is a possible tendency for the test to be more likely to mix multiple objectives. To avoid this, this rule reports when the maximum number of assertions is exceeded.Why is this bad? ​This rule enforces a maximum number of expect() calls. The following patterns are considered warnings (with the default option of { "max": 5 }):Examples ​Examples of incorrect code for this rule:javascripttest("should not pass", () => {
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
});

it("should not pass", () => {
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
  expect(true).toBeDefined();
});How to use ​To enable this rule in the CLI or using the config file, you can use:CLIConfig (.oxlintrc.json)bashoxlint --deny jest/max-expects --jest-pluginjson{
  "plugins": ["jest"],
  "rules": {
    "jest/max-expects": "error"
  }
}References ​Rule Source

