promise/no-nesting Style ​🚧 An auto-fix is still under development. What it does ​Disallow nested then() or catch() statements.Why is this bad? ​Nesting promises makes code harder to read and understand.Examples ​Examples of incorrect code for this rule:javascriptdoThing().then(() => a.then());

doThing().then(function() {
  a.then();
});

doThing().then(() => {
  b.catch();
});

doThing().catch((val) => doSomething(val).catch(errors));Examples of correct code for this rule:javascriptdoThing().then(() => 4);

doThing().then(function() {
  return 4;
});

doThing().catch(() => 4);javascriptdoThing()
  .then(() => Promise.resolve(1))
  .then(() => Promise.resolve(2));This example is not a rule violation as unnesting here would result in a being undefined in the expression getC(a, b).javascriptdoThing()
  .then(a =>
    getB(a)
      .then(b => getC(a, b))
  );How to use ​To enable this rule in the CLI or using the config file, you can use:CLIConfig (.oxlintrc.json)bashoxlint --deny promise/no-nesting --promise-pluginjson{
  "plugins": ["promise"],
  "rules": {
    "promise/no-nesting": "error"
  }
}References ​Rule Source

