typescript/no-empty-interface Style ​What it does ​Disallow the declaration of empty interfaces.Why is this bad? ​An empty interface in TypeScript does very little: any non-nullable value is assignable to {}. Using an empty interface is often a sign of programmer error, such as misunderstanding the concept of {} or forgetting to fill in fields. This rule aims to ensure that only meaningful interfaces are declared in the code.Examples ​Examples of incorrect code for this rule:tsinterface Foo {}
interface Bar extends Foo {}Examples of correct code for this rule:tsinterface Foo {
  member: string;
}
interface Bar extends Foo {
  member: string;
}How to use ​To enable this rule in the CLI or using the config file, you can use:CLIConfig (.oxlintrc.json)bashoxlint --deny typescript/no-empty-interfacejson{
  "rules": {
    "typescript/no-empty-interface": "error"
  }
}References ​Rule Source

