typescript/no-inferrable-types Style ​🚧 An auto-fix is still under development. What it does ​Disallow explicit type declarations for variables or parameters initialized to a number, string, or booleanWhy is this bad? ​Explicitly typing variables or parameters that are initialized to a literal value is unnecessary because TypeScript can infer the type from the value.Examples ​Examples of incorrect code for this rule:tsconst a: number = 5;
const b: string = "foo";
const c: boolean = true;
const fn = (a: number = 5, b: boolean = true, c: string = "foo") => {};Examples of correct code for this rule:tsconst a = 5;
const b = "foo";
const c = true;
const fn = (a = 5, b = true, c = "foo") => {};How to use ​To enable this rule in the CLI or using the config file, you can use:CLIConfig (.oxlintrc.json)bashoxlint --deny typescript/no-inferrable-typesjson{
  "rules": {
    "typescript/no-inferrable-types": "error"
  }
}References ​Rule Source

